A **tree** is a hierarchical data structure consisting of nodes, where each node has a single root and can have multiple children. Here are the key features and operations associated with trees based on the document:

- **Hierarchical Structure**: Data is organized in a parent-child relationship.
- **Dynamic Size**: Trees can grow and shrink as nodes are added or removed.

### Types of Trees
- **Binary Tree**: A specific type of tree where each node has at most two children, referred to as the left and right child.

### Example Code for a Binary Tree
```java
class TreeNode {
    int data;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;
    TreeNode(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        left = null;
        right = null;
    }
}
class BinaryTree {
    TreeNode root;
    public void insert(int data) {
        root = insertRec(root, data);
    }
    private TreeNode insertRec(TreeNode root, int data) {
        if (root == null) {
            root = new TreeNode(data);
            return root;
        }
        if (data < root.data) {
            root.left = insertRec(root.left, data);
        } else if (data > root.data) {
            root.right = insertRec(root.right, data);
        }
        return root;
    }
}
```
*(Source: Pages 7-8)*

### Operations on Trees
- **Insertion**: Typically takes O(n) time on average for balanced trees.
- **Traversal**: Can be performed using depth-first methods (in-order, pre-order, post-order) or breadth-first (level order).

